    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d239">

                <pb facs="0460" n="430" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v34/pg430"/>

                <head>239. Telegram From the Embassy in Libya to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d239-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Tripoli</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1967-06-11T12:00:00+00:00">June 11, 1967,
                    1200Z</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>21. Libyan Oil Sitrep—June 10 and 11.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Petroleum Minister Khaliifah Muusa departed morning June 11 for Baida,
                        where he hopes to convince Prime Minister Hussain Maaziq to allow resumption
                        of production and export to countries other than U.S. and U.K. According to
                        Esso Libya President Hugh Wynne (protect source) Muusa was surprised on his
                        return from Baghdad to learn GOL had banned totally all production and
                        exports, thus going beyond scope of Baghdad resolution which banned exports
                        only to countries aiding Israel.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d239-f2"/>
                    </item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>To best Embassy’s knowledge, there has been no destruction yet of oil
                        installations, and expatriate staff at least are standing by. <pb
                            facs="0461" n="431" xml:id="pg431"/> Operations apparently could be
                        resumed within hours of GOL approval.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Morning June 10 bomb thrown into Tripoli warehouse of Geophysical Service
                        International (GSI). There were no personal injuries, but valuable seismic
                        records were destroyed.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Saturday (June 10) most oil company offices in Tripoli reopened for
                        business. Libyan absenteeism higher than previous, but union has ordered
                        Libyans return to work on Monday.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold"> Newsom </hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
